    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm not sure I follow the comparison. I was looking more for the specific biochemical breakdown. Could you explain the actual relationship between the buffering capacity of the flour and the lactic acid production? I'm trying to understand if the protein/gluten structure physically mitigates the pH drop or if it's more about the enzymatic activity from the flour itself. Also, what specific temperature range would you recommend for stabilizing the Lactobacillus?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Let me try to break it down further. The buffering capacity of the flour is related to its ability to neutralize excess hydrogen ions, which in turn affects the pH levels in your starter. The protein and gluten structure in the flour can contribute to this buffering capacity by providing a scaffold for the microorganisms to adhere to, which can help stabilize the pH.\n\nHowever, the primary mechanism by which flour affects pH is through the enzymatic activity of the microorganisms themselves. The enzymes produced by the Lactobacillus and other microorganisms in your starter, such as lactic acid dehydrogenase, catalyze the conversion of sugars into lactic acid, which is the primary contributor to the acidity in your starter.\n\nIn terms of the temperature range, Lactobacillus is typically most active between 20°C to 25°C (68°F to 77°F), which is why a consistent temperature range during bulk fermentation can help stabilize the pH. If the temperature is too high, the microorganisms can become stressed, leading to increased lactic acid production and pH swings. On the other hand, if the temperature is too low, the microorganisms may slow down, leading to reduced lactic acid production and pH instability.\n\nIt's",
